    "taskResult": "Done.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nAdded `heartbeat(lease_id, renew, sleep, should_continue)` to `worker.py` (repo root) and renamed nothing else:\n\n```python\n_RENEWAL_INTERVAL_SECONDS = 20\n\ndef heartbeat(lease_id, renew, sleep, should_continue) -> None:\n    while should_continue():\n        renew(lease_id)\n        sleep(_RENEWAL_INTERVAL_SECONDS)\n```\n\n- Exact signature preserved.\n- Loop condition is `should_continue()`; each iteration renews, then sleeps the interval before the next renewal.\n- No threads, no `time`/wall-clock calls, no work at import (module level is only an `int` constant).\n\n## Interval choice, from the dated records\n\nI distinguished earlier proposals from the later decisions:\n\n| Record | Date | Assistant proposal | User decision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `p01.jsonl` | 2026-04-12 | 60s | **90s** |\n| `p02.jsonl` | 2026-06-02 | 30–45s | **45s** |\n| `p03.jsonl` | 2026-07-21 | 30s | **20s** (final) |\n\nThe latest decision (p03, closing user turn) supersedes the 90s and 45s decisions and the p03 assistant's 30s proposal: *\"the worker will renew every 20 seconds… 20 seconds is the final renewal interval.\"* The current `lease.py` confirms the broker TTL is 120s measured from the last successful renewal, so 20s leaves wide headroom. I used **20s**.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Imported the module and confirmed no import-time side effects.\n- Drove `heartbeat` with instrumented `renew`/`sleep`/`should_continue`: got 3 renewals of the exact lease id and sleeps of `[20, 20, 20]`, then it stopped when `should_continue()` went false.\n- AST check: imports are only `__future__` and `lease` — no `threading`, no `time`.\n\nI left the rest of `worker.py` (docstring and `drain`) untouched, since the task only asked for the helper.",
